<\body>
  <doc-data|<doc-title|<cqft-course-name>>|<doc-subtitle|Lecture<nbsp>4:
  Forward\Ubackward equations>|<doc-author|<cqft-author-data>>|<doc-date|SISSA,
  November 2026>>

  <abstract-data|<\abstract>
    Building on the scale decomposition and the stochastic observables of
    Lecture<nbsp>3, we introduce interactions. The interacting measure can be
    generated in three ways: as a renormalisation group decomposition of the
    covariance, as an optimal control problem, whose dynamic programming
    equation is the Polchinski equation, and as a filtering problem: the
    field at resolution <math|t> is a noisy observation of the terminal
    field, the Polchinski process is this observation written in its own
    filtration, and backward in resolution its dynamics is linear, with
    Gaussian transitions, started from the interacting measure. The three
    views meet in one forward\Ubackward system for a field and a force, and
    its natural coordinates are the force coordinates: the forward equation
    loses its drift, and the conditional covariance becomes the propagator.
    All this can be said in terms of forces, propagators and covariances. In
    the gradient case there is a parallel story for potentials: the value
    function generates the canonical transformation to force coordinates, the
    Legendre transform of the log-partition function of the filter gives the
    effective average action, and its flow is the Wetterich equation.
    Lecture<nbsp>5 continues with the observables of the interacting theory,
    the Euclidean field equation and Nelson's Euclidean noise, the field
    equation at every resolution with its sub-Gaussian noise, and infinite
    volume, and contains the appendices. The treatment is formal: we compute
    as if all objects were smooth and integrable, and we indicate where the
    real difficulties lie.

    The results presented in Lectures<nbsp>4\U7 were obtained recently, with
    the use of AI, in collaboration with Sarah-Jean Meyer.
  </abstract>>

