  <section*|Introduction>

  In Lecture<nbsp>3 we decomposed the Gaussian free field along a resolution
  parameter and took as primary objects the stochastic observables:
  martingales in the filtration of finite-resolution measurements
  <cite|BCG26>. In this lecture we introduce interactions, and we tell one
  story from three points of view: renormalisation, optimal control and
  filtering. See also <cite|Pol84|BK87|Sal99|BBD24> for the renormalisation
  group side and <cite|BG20|BG23> for the variational side.

  <paragraph|Structure> Section<nbsp><reference|sec:polchinski> generates the
  interacting measure in the three ways, and shows that they produce the same
  field and the same force. Section<nbsp><reference|sec:wetterich> finds the
  natural coordinates, first in terms of forces, propagators and covariances,
  then in terms of potentials, where the value function generates the
  canonical transformation and the Legendre transform leads to the Wetterich
  equation. Lecture<nbsp>5 continues with observables, the Euclidean field
  equation and Nelson's Euclidean noise, the field equation at every
  resolution with its sub-Gaussian noise, the Dyson\USchwinger equations,
  also in pathwise form, and infinite volume, and contains the appendices:
  the algebra of the flow, perturbation theory and counterterms, and the
  functional inequalities that follow from the localisation.

  <paragraph|What is formal> Rigorous statements require a finite-dimensional
  setting: a finite lattice, or a Fourier cutoff on the torus, together with
  a finite terminal resolution <math|T>. There, all the identities below are
  theorems under mild growth assumptions on the interaction, and their proofs
  are the computations we give. The hard problems are the limits
  <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> (ultraviolet) and infinite volume (infrared),
  together with the locality of the limiting objects. We mark with
  <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> the statements that we only derive at this
  level.

  <paragraph|Conventions> As in Lectures<nbsp>1\U3:
  <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>> or <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|d>>,
  <math|A=m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>>, <math|C=A<rsup|-1>> with kernel <math|G>,
  <math|\<mu\><rsub|m>> the GFF, <math|<around|\<langle\>|f,g|\<rangle\>>=<big|int>f*g>
  for real functions. For a functional <math|F> on smooth fields,
  <math|\<mathD\>F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>\<in\>\<cal-S\><rprime|'><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
  and <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>F<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>> are its first and
  second derivatives, <math|\<mathD\><rsub|h>*F=<around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>F,h|\<rangle\>>>.
  <math|H<rsub|n>*<around|(|a;c|)>> are the Hermite polynomials of variance
  <math|c>, and <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q>> is the centred Gaussian measure with
  covariance <math|Q>.

  \;

  <paragraph|Acknowledgements>The results presented in Lectures<nbsp>4\U7 are
  joint work with Sarah-Jean Meyer, obtained recently with the use of AI.
  M.<nbsp>Gubinelli is partly supported by UK Research and Innovation via the
