  \PStochFields\Q EP/Z534328/1.

  <section*|Main lecture>

  <assign|section-nr|6><section|Three ways to generate the interacting
  measure><label|sec:polchinski>

  Fix a finite volume <math|\<cal-M\>=\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>> and a
  terminal resolution <math|T\<less\>\<infty\>>, which plays the role of an
  ultraviolet cutoff: the reference field
  <math|X<rsub|T>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>>> is smooth. Let
  <math|V<rsub|T>> be an interaction, a functional of smooth fields bounded
  below. Our main example is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:phi4-VT>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<big|int><rsub|\<bbb-T\><rsup|d><rsub|L>><around*|[|<frac|\<lambda\>|12>*H<rsub|4>*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|T>|)>+\<gamma\><rsub|T>*<space|0.17em>H<rsub|2>*<around|(|\<varphi\><around|(|x|)>;c<rsub|T>|)>|]>\<mathd\>x,
  </equation>

  with a mass counterterm <math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>> to be fixed in
  Appendix<nbsp><hlink|A.2|cqft_lecture_5.tm#sec:pert> of Lecture<nbsp>5
  (<math|\<gamma\><rsub|T>=0> for <math|d=2>). The coupling is normalised by
  <math|\<lambda\>/12> so that the curvature of the quartic term is
  <math|\<lambda\>*\<varphi\><rsup|2>>, as in Lectures<nbsp>6\U7. The
  interacting measure at cutoff <math|T> is

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:nuT>\<nu\><rsub|T><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>.
  </equation>

  How does the Gaussian scale decomposition of Lecture<nbsp>3 generate
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>? There are three answers.

  <\itemize>
    <item><strong|Renormalisation> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:rg>). Split
    the covariance into shells and integrate them out one at a time. At every
    resolution the field is again a Gibbs measure, and it is driven by an
    effective force.

    <item><strong|Optimal control> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:control>).
    Steer the Gaussian field by a drift, at the least cost. The effective
    potential is the value function, and the Polchinski equation is the
    dynamic programming equation.

    <item><strong|Filtering> (Section<nbsp><reference|sec:filtering>).
    Observe the terminal field through noise, with increasing precision. The
    field at resolution <math|t> is the observation, the force is what it
    reveals about the terminal field, and backward in resolution the process
    has a linear dynamics with Gaussian transitions, started from the
    interacting measure.
  </itemize>

  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:meet> shows that the three constructions
  produce the same process. Throughout,

  <\equation*>
    S<rsub|t>\<assign\>Q<rsub|T>-Q<rsub|t>=Q<rsub|T,t>,<space|2em><wide|S|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>,<space|2em>S<rsub|T>=0,
  </equation*>

  is the covariance still unresolved at resolution <math|t>.

  <subsection|Renormalisation: decomposing the covariance><label|sec:rg>

  Decompose the covariance as in Lecture<nbsp>3:
  <math|Q<rsub|T>=Q<rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>>, and correspondingly

  <\equation*>
    X<rsub|T>=X<rsub|t>+<around|(|X<rsub|T>-X<rsub|t>|)>
  </equation*>

  with independent terms. Integrating out the second one defines the
  effective potential.

  <\definition>
    The <em|effective potential> at resolution <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,T|]>>
    is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:Vt>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<assign\><big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>*<around|(|\<varphi\>+\<zeta\>|)>><space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<zeta\>|)>.
    </equation>
  </definition>

  <math|V<rsub|t>> is the interaction felt by the field at resolution
  <math|t> once the fluctuations between <math|t> and <math|T> have been
  integrated out: it is the Wilson effective action. At <math|t=0>,
  <math|V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=-log Z<rsub|T>> is the free energy. Here is
  the first key fact.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Marginals><label|prop:marginals> Let <math|\<bbb-P\>> be defined
    by

    <\equation*>
      <frac|\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\>|\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>>,
    </equation*>

    where <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>> is the law of the Gaussian process
    <math|X>. Then <math|<math-up|Law><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>=\<nu\><rsub|T>>,
    and for every <math|t\<leqslant\>T>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:nut><math-up|Law><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>=\<nu\><rsub|t>,<space|2em>\<nu\><rsub|t><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>\<assign\>Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>.
    </equation>

    Moreover, the conditional law of <math|X<rsub|T>> given
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>> is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:localisation>\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|X<rsub|t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>,<space|2em>\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>><around|(|G|)>\<assign\>\<mathe\><rsup|V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>><big|int>G*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>*<around|(|\<phi\>+\<zeta\>|)>><space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<zeta\>|)>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Under <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>>, <math|X<rsub|T>=X<rsub|t>+<around|(|X<rsub|T>-X<rsub|t>|)>>
    with an independent increment of law <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T,t>>>.
    Hence <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<bbb-P\>><around|[|G<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>|]>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around|[|G<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>>|]>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around|[|G<around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>>|]>>.
    The conditional law follows in the same way.
  </proof>

  So at every resolution the field is again a Gibbs measure, with reference
  covariance <math|Q<rsub|t>> and potential <math|V<rsub|t>>. The
  measure-valued martingale <math|<around|(|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|X<rsub|t>>|)><rsub|t>>
  goes from <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> at <math|t=0> to
  <math|\<delta\><rsub|X<rsub|T>>> at <math|t=T>: it is a <em|stochastic
  localisation> of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> in the sense of
  Eldan<nbsp><cite|Eld23>, adapted to the covariance
  profile<nbsp><cite|BBD24|BCG26>.

  <paragraph|The field as a process> The density of the interacting law with
  respect to the Gaussian one is a martingale in resolution, so by Girsanov
  the field acquires a drift, and this drift is the effective force. The
  resulting process is the <strong|Polchinski process>.

  <\key-result>
    <\theorem>
      <dueto|FBSDE for the interacting field><label|thm:fbsde>
      <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> Under <math|\<bbb-P\>> the process
      <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>\<assign\>X<rsub|t>>, the <em|interacting field at
      resolution <math|t>>, solves

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:fbsde>\<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>F<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>*<around*|[|-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>,
      </equation>

      with <math|\<Phi\><rsub|0>=0> and a <math|\<bbb-P\>>-cylindrical
      Brownian motion <math|W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>>>. In particular the
      <em|effective force> <math|<around|(|F<rsub|t>|)><rsub|t\<leqslant\>T>>
      is a <math|\<bbb-P\>>-martingale: an observable field of the
      interacting theory.
    </theorem>
  </key-result>

  <\proof>
    The density process is <math|M<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around|[|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\>/<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>\|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|]>=Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>>>,
    by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marginals>. It is a martingale, so
    the drift in Itô's formula vanishes and <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    M<rsub|t>=-M<rsub|t><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    W<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>>. By Girsanov's theorem,
    <math|W<rsup|\<bbb-P\>><rsub|t>=W<rsub|t>+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>\<mathD\>V<rsub|s><around|(|X<rsub|s>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    s> is a <math|\<bbb-P\>>-Brownian motion, which is
    (<reference|eq:fbsde>). Differentiating (<reference|eq:Vt>) gives
    <math|\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>=\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>><around|(|\<mathD\>V<rsub|T>|)>>,
    which is the second identity.
  </proof>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|A Doob <math|h>-transform><label|rem:doob-h> Under
    <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>>, <math|X> is the GFF martingale of
    Lecture<nbsp>3, <math|X<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around|[|X<rsub|T>\|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|]>>,
    a Gaussian process with independent increments and generator
    <math|L<rsub|t>=<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>|)>>.
    The function

    <\equation*>
      h<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>\<assign\>Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around*|[|Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>><mid|\|>X<rsub|t>=\<phi\>|]>
    </equation*>

    is space-time harmonic for <math|X>, <math|<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|t>+L<rsub|t>|)>*h<rsub|t>=0>,
    and this is the Polchinski equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski> for
    <math|V<rsub|t>=-log h<rsub|t>-log Z<rsub|T>>. Since
    <math|h<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=1>, the density of <math|\<bbb-P\>> with
    respect to <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>> on <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>> is
    <math|h<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>>: the Polchinski process is the
    <em|Doob <math|h>-transform> of the GFF martingale by the terminal weight
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>>>. Its generator is

    <\equation*>
      L<rsub|t><rsup|h>*G=h<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|\<partial\><rsub|t>+L<rsub|t>|)><around|(|h<rsub|t>*G|)>-\<partial\><rsub|t>*G=L<rsub|t>*G+<around|\<langle\>|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\>log
      h<rsub|t>,\<mathD\>G|\<rangle\>>,
    </equation*>

    and the force is the logarithmic gradient of <math|h>,
    <math|F<rsub|t>=\<mathD\>log h<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>.
    Conditioning a Brownian motion on its endpoint is the simplest example:
    <math|h<rsub|t>> is then the Gaussian density of the remaining increment,
    and the drift is that of the bridge<nbsp><eqref|eq:observation>. The
    interacting field is a GFF martingale conditioned, in the sense of Doob,
    to end with the law <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>. The same transform acts on the
    backward Kolmogorov equation<nbsp><hlink|(9.2)|cqft_lecture_5.tm#eq:kolmogorov>
    of Lecture<nbsp>5 (Exercise<nbsp><hlink|9.1|cqft_lecture_5.tm#ex:doob>
    there).
  </remark>

  Read as an equation for an unknown pair
  <math|<around|(|\<Phi\>,F|)>>,<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> is a
  <em|forward\Ubackward> system<nbsp><cite|MY99>. The field is driven forward
  in resolution by a force that is determined backward, as the conditional
  expectation of a functional of the terminal field:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:fbsde-system>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*F<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>,<space|2em>F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around*|[|-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>,<space|2em>t\<in\><around|[|0,T|]>.
  </equation>

  Here and below we drop the superscript and write <math|W> for the Brownian
  motion driving the interacting field. No equation for the potential is
  needed to state<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde-system>, and
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>=<math-up|Law><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>> is
  recovered from the solution. This is the starting point of FBSDE
  constructions of Euclidean fields (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:fbsde-flow>):
  one solves<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde-system> with renormalised <math|V<rsub|T>>,
  with estimates uniform in <math|T> and in the volume, and takes the limit.
  The field coordinate <math|\<Phi\>> is <em|not> an observable, only a
  semimartingale. Its martingale part is the Gaussian noise, and the
  observables are built from it and from <math|F>.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|fbsde_flow.png>|0.9par|||><label|fig:fbsde-flow>>|The
  forward\Ubackward structure of<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde-system> on
  <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|2>>, heat profile, at resolutions
  <math|t<rsub|1>\<less\>t<rsub|2>\<less\>t<rsub|3>\<less\>T>, all built from
  the same noise. Top: the field gains the fluctuation of each shell,
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>>, and reveals
  finer modes. Read backward, the same process solves the linear
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:reversal>: a Gaussian blurring of
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|T>>, which does not see the
  interaction (dashed arrow). Vertical arrows: at each resolution the force
  is the function <math|-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>> of the field. Bottom: the
  effective force <math|F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|[|-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>>
  for the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> interaction, determined backward from the
  terminal interaction. It is shown to first order in <math|\<lambda\>>,
  where it is the Wick cube <math|-<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*H<rsub|3>*<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>;c<rsub|t>|)>>,
  and each panel is in its own units.>

  <subsection|Optimal control: the Polchinski equation><label|sec:control>

  The measure <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> also solves a minimisation problem.
  Perturb the Gaussian field by an adapted drift, and pay for the drift and
  for the interaction at the endpoint. The effective potential is the value
  function of this problem. Its equation in <math|t>, which we first derive
  directly from (<reference|eq:Vt>), is the dynamic programming equation.

  <\key-result>
    <\theorem>
      <dueto|Polchinski equation<nbsp><cite|Pol84>><label|thm:polchinski>
      <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> <math|V<rsub|t>> solves the backward
      Hamilton\UJacobi equation

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:polchinski>\<partial\><rsub|t>*V<rsub|t>=<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t>|)>,<space|2em>t\<in\><around|[|0,T|]>,
      </equation>

      with terminal condition <math|V<rsub|T>>.
    </theorem>
  </key-result>

  <\proof>
    <math|u<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t>>=\<mathe\><rsup|<frac|1|2>*\<Delta\><rsub|Q<rsub|T,t>>>u<rsub|T>>
    is a Gaussian convolution, where <math|\<Delta\><rsub|Q>\<assign\><math-up|Tr><around|(|Q*\<mathD\><rsup|2>|)>>.
    Since <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*Q<rsub|T,t>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>,
    it solves the backward heat equation <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*u<rsub|t>=-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>u<rsub|t>|)>>.
    Insert <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>u=<around|(|\<mathD\>V\<otimes\>\<mathD\>V-\<mathD\><rsup|2>V|)>*u>
    and divide by <math|-u>.
  </proof>

  The nonlinear term generates new, generally nonlocal, interactions (trees),
  and the trace term contracts pairs of fields with the covariance of the
  shell <math|<around|[|t,t+\<mathd\>t|]>> (loops). The substitution
  <math|u=\<mathe\><rsup|-V>>, which linearises the equation, is a Cole\UHopf
  transform: we come back to this in Section<nbsp><reference|sec:burgers>,
  and in Appendix<nbsp><hlink|A|cqft_lecture_5.tm#sec:algebra> of
  Lecture<nbsp>5.

  <paragraph|The control problem> Let <math|\<bbb-H\><rsub|a>> be the set of
  progressively measurable drifts <math|u:<around|[|0,T|]>\<to\>L<rsup|2><around|(|\<cal-M\>|)>>
  with <math|\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s\<less\>\<infty\>>,
  and set

  <\equation*>
    I<rsub|t><around|(|u|)>\<assign\><big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*u<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s,<space|2em>X<rsup|u>\<assign\>X+I<around|(|u|)>,
  </equation*>

  the field driven by the control <math|u> under the Gaussian reference
  <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>>.

  The following result is standard in the stochastic optimal control setting,
  see e.g. Fleming and Soner's book<nbsp><cite|FS06>. A useful non-Markovian
  generalisation is given by Boué\UDupuis<nbsp><cite|BD98>.

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|Polchinski is HJB><label|thm:BD> <with|font-shape|right|(formal)>

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:BD>-log \<bbb-E\><rsub|0><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>>|]>=V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>=inf<rsub|u\<in\>\<bbb-H\><rsub|a>>
      \<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<around*|[|V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s|]>.
    </equation>

    The infimum is attained at the feedback control
    <math|u<rsup|\<ast\>><rsub|s>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*\<mathD\>V<rsub|s><around|(|X<rsup|u<rsup|\<ast\>>><rsub|s>|)>>,
    and <math|<math-up|Law><around|(|X<rsup|u<rsup|\<ast\>>><rsub|T>|)>=\<nu\><rsub|T>>.
    The effective potential is the value function,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski> is the Hamilton\UJacobi\UBellman equation
    of this control problem.
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    (Verification.) For any <math|u>, by Itô's formula
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski>,

    <\align*>
      <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|t>|)>>|<cell|=<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>+<around|\<langle\>|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>,u<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>|]>\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\>M<rsub|t>>>|<row|<cell|>|<cell|=<around*|[|<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|t>+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>-<tfrac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>|]>\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\>M<rsub|t>,>>>>
    </align*>

    with <math|\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>=\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|t>|)>>
    and a martingale <math|M>. Integrating and taking expectations,

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:verification>\<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<around*|[|V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|t>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>t|]>=V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>+<frac|1|2>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|t>+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|t>|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>t.
    </equation>

    So the cost is <math|\<geqslant\>V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>>, with equality
    iff <math|u=u<rsup|\<ast\>>>. With this control
    <math|X<rsup|u<rsup|\<ast\>>>> solves the same SDE as <math|\<Phi\>>
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Euler\ULagrange equation <math|=> FBSDE> Perturbing
  <math|u\<to\>u+\<varepsilon\>*v> in<nbsp><eqref|eq:BD>, the first-order
  condition is <math|\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<langle\>|u<rsub|s>+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*<around|[|\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>|]>,v<rsub|s>|\<rangle\>>\<mathd\>s=0>
  for all adapted <math|v>, i.e.

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:EL>u<rsub|s>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*<space|0.17em>\<bbb-E\><rsub|s>*<around*|[|\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>|]>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*F<rsub|s>.
  </equation>

  This is<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde-system>. The FBSDE is the Euler\ULagrange
  equation of the variational problem, and the Polchinski equation is its
  Hamilton\UJacobi equation.

  <paragraph|Entropy> By Girsanov, <math|<frac|1|2>*\<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<big|int><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<geqslant\><math-up|Ent><around|(|<math-up|Law><around|(|X<rsup|u>|)><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em><math-up|Law><around|(|X|)>|)>>,
  which is at least the entropy of the terminal marginal.
  So<nbsp><eqref|eq:BD> is a pathwise lift of the Gibbs variational principle
  <math|-log Z<rsub|T>=inf<rsub|\<nu\>><around|{|<big|int>V<rsub|T><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<nu\>+<math-up|Ent><around|(|\<nu\>\|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>>|)>|}>>.
  The lift is useful because adapted drifts can be <em|constructed scale by
  scale>: one looks for <math|u> as a functional of the Gaussian process
  <math|X>, not as an unknown measure.

  <paragraph|Generating functionals> Adding a test functional <math|f>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:generating>\<cal-W\><rsub|T><around|(|f|)>\<assign\>-log
    <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-f>\<mathd\>\<nu\><rsub|T>=inf<rsub|u>
    \<bbb-E\><rsub|0>*<around*|[|f<around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>+V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s|]>-V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>.
  </equation>

  Bounds on <math|\<cal-W\><rsub|T><around|(|f|)>> uniform in <math|T>, for
  <math|f> in a rich class, give tightness, moment bounds and the
  identification of limits. For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> the naive
  problem does not have uniform bounds: the leading-order optimal drift
  <math|u<rsub|s>\<approx\>-<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>:X<rsub|s><rsup|3>>:
  has a divergent cost. One renormalises by the change of variables
  <math|u=-<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>:X<rsup|3>:+r>.
  After it, and after further paracontrolled changes of variables, the
  singular terms are explicit polynomials in the Wick powers of <math|X>,
  cancelled by <em|the counterterms>, and the residual functional is
  coercive. Its control in Besov and paracontrolled norms, uniformly in
  <math|T>, is the construction of <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>>
  in<nbsp><cite|BG20>; see also<nbsp><cite|BG21>.

  <paragraph|Pontryagin's maximum principle> Consider first a deterministic
  problem: minimise the cost <math|J<around|(|u|)>=g<around|(|x<rsub|T>|)>+<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|T>\<ell\><around|(|x<rsub|s>,u<rsub|s>|)>*\<mathd\>s>
  over controls <math|u> on <math|<around|[|t,T|]>>, where the state solves
  <math|<wide|x|\<dot\>><rsub|s>=b<around|(|x<rsub|s>,u<rsub|s>|)>>,
  <math|x<rsub|t>=x>. With the <em|Hamiltonian>

  <\equation*>
    H<around|(|x,p,u|)>\<assign\><around|\<langle\>|p,b<around|(|x,u|)>|\<rangle\>>-\<ell\><around|(|x,u|)>,
  </equation*>

  Pontryagin's principle<nbsp><cite|PBGM62> says that along an optimal pair
  <math|<around|(|x,u|)>> there is a <em|costate> <math|p> such that

  <\equation*>
    <wide|x|\<dot\>><rsub|s>=\<partial\><rsub|p>*H,<space|2em><wide|p|\<dot\>><rsub|s>=-\<partial\><rsub|x>*H,<space|2em>p<rsub|T>=-\<mathD\>g<around|(|x<rsub|T>|)>,<space|2em>u<rsub|s>\<in\>arg
    max<rsub|v> H<around|(|x<rsub|s>,p<rsub|s>,v|)>.
  </equation*>

  The state runs forward from its initial value, the costate backward from
  its terminal value: a two-point boundary value problem. In the smooth case
  the principle is the first-order condition. If
  <math|\<xi\>=\<partial\><rsub|\<varepsilon\>>*x> is the variation of the
  state under <math|u\<to\>u+\<varepsilon\>*v>, then
  <math|<wide|\<xi\>|\<dot\>>=\<partial\><rsub|x>*b*\<xi\>+\<partial\><rsub|u>*b*v>,
  <math|\<xi\><rsub|t>=0>, and the costate equation is exactly what makes
  <math|<frac|\<mathd\>|\<mathd\>s><around|\<langle\>|p,\<xi\>|\<rangle\>>=<around|\<langle\>|\<partial\><rsub|x>*\<ell\>,\<xi\>|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|p,\<partial\><rsub|u>*b*v|\<rangle\>>>.
  Integrating over <math|<around|[|t,T|]>> and using the terminal condition,

  <\equation*>
    <frac|\<mathd\>|\<mathd\>\<varepsilon\>>J<around|(|u+\<varepsilon\>*v|)><mid|\|><rsub|\<varepsilon\>=0>=-<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|T><around|\<langle\>|\<partial\><rsub|u>*H<around|(|x<rsub|s>,p<rsub|s>,u<rsub|s>|)>,v<rsub|s>|\<rangle\>>*\<mathd\>s.
  </equation*>

  So the costate is the Lagrange multiplier of the constraint
  <math|<wide|x|\<dot\>>=b>, and an optimal control is a critical point of
  the Hamiltonian.

  <paragraph|The costate is a gradient> Let
  <math|V<around|(|t,x|)>=min<rsub|u> J<around|(|u|)>> be the value function.
  Comparing the cost from <math|<around|(|t,x|)>> with the cost from
  <math|<around|(|t+\<mathd\>t,x+b*\<mathd\>t|)>> gives the
  Hamilton\UJacobi\UBellman equation

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|t>*V=H<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x,-\<mathD\>V|)>,<space|2em>H<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|x,p|)>\<assign\>max<rsub|u>
    H<around|(|x,p,u|)>,<space|2em>V<around|(|T,\<cdummy\>|)>=g.
  </equation*>

  Its characteristics are Hamilton's equations for <math|H<rsub|\<ast\>>>,
  and along them <math|p<rsub|s>=-\<mathD\>V<around|(|s,x<rsub|s>|)>>: the
  costate is the sensitivity of the optimal cost to a displacement of the
  state, i.e. the force that the future exerts on the present.

  <paragraph|The Hamiltonian of the field> In the problem of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:BD> the state is the field,
  <math|b<around|(|x,u|)>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*u>,
  <math|\<ell\>=<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>> and
  <math|g=V<rsub|T>>. Then <math|H<around|(|p,u|)>=<around|\<langle\>|p,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*u|\<rangle\>>-<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>
  is maximal at <math|u=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>*p>, and

  <\equation*>
    H<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|p|)>=<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|p,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*p|\<rangle\>>
  </equation*>

  is the Hamiltonian of a free particle with mass matrix
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|-1>>: it does not depend on the state.
  Hence <math|<wide|p|\<dot\>>=0> and <math|<wide|x|\<dot\>>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>*p>:
  the costate is conserved, and from <math|t> to <math|T> the state moves
  along the linear shear

  <\equation>
    \<Sigma\><rsub|t>:<around|(|x,p|)>\<longmapsto\><around|(|x+S<rsub|t>*p,<space|0.27em>p|)>.<label|eq:shear>
  </equation>

  Its trajectories are the characteristics of the transport part
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:burgers>. All the interaction sits in the terminal
  condition <math|p=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|x+S<rsub|t>*p|)>>, an
  implicit equation for the momentum, solved by a sum of tree graphs
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:characteristics>). The corresponding
  Hamilton\UJacobi equation <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*V=<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>V,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\>V|\<rangle\>>>
  is the Polchinski equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski> without its trace
  term.

  <paragraph|With noise> For a controlled diffusion
  <math|\<mathd\>X=b<around|(|X,u|)>*\<mathd\>s+\<sigma\>*\<mathd\>W>, with
  <math|\<sigma\>> independent of the control, the stochastic maximum
  principle<nbsp><cite|Bis73|Pen90|YZ99> keeps the Hamiltonian and the
  maximisation, but the costate becomes an adapted process which solves a
  <em|backward> stochastic differential equation,

  <\equation*>
    \<mathd\>p<rsub|s>=-\<partial\><rsub|x>*H*<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+\<Upsilon\><rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|s>,<space|2em>p<rsub|T>=-\<mathD\>g<around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>.
  </equation*>

  The martingale term <math|\<Upsilon\>*\<mathd\>W> is the price of
  adaptedness: the costate may not anticipate the noise. For the field
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|x>*H=0>, so the costate is a martingale,
  <math|p<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsub|T>|)>|]>>,
  and since the value function is the effective potential
  (Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:BD>), <math|p<rsub|t>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around|(|X<rsub|t>|)>>.
  The noise adds the Itô term to the Hamilton\UJacobi equation, which becomes
  the Polchinski equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski>.

  In the notation of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:BD> the controlled field is
  <math|\<mathd\>X=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*<around|(|u<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\>W|)>>
  and the optimal control is <math|u<rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*p<rsub|t>>
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:pontryagin>). Since <math|p<rsub|t>> is the
  value <math|F<rsub|t>> of the force, the FBSDE<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde-system>
  is the stochastic Pontryagin system of this problem:

  <\padded-center>
    <em|the field <math|\<Phi\>> is the state, and the force <math|F> is the
    costate.>
  </padded-center>

  <subsection|Filtering: measuring the field><label|sec:filtering>

  Stochastic localisation was introduced by Eldan<nbsp><cite|Eld13> to study
  measures in high dimension (see<nbsp><cite|Eld23> for a survey). The idea
  is to write a measure as a mixture of simpler ones, through a
  measure-valued martingale driven by noise.
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marginals> says that the Polchinski
  process does exactly this for <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>. What Eldan's
  construction adds to the picture of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:rg> is a
  statistical reading:\ 

  <\padded-center>
    <\with|font-shape|italic>
      the Polchinski process is a noisy observation of its own terminal
      value, and its drift is what an optimal filter learns from this
      observation.
    </with>
  </padded-center>

  <paragraph|The terminal field as a hidden signal> Assume that all the
  deterministic covariance operators are functions of <math|A>, hence
  commute, and let <math|K=Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>>, <math|J<rsub|t>=K*Q<rsub|t>>,
  so that <math|J<rsub|0>=0>, <math|J<rsub|T>=1>. For the heat profile,
  <math|K=A\<mathe\><rsup|A/T<rsup|2>>> and
  <math|J<rsub|t>=\<mathe\><rsup|-A<around|(|t<rsup|-2>-T<rsup|-2>|)>>> is a
  heat semigroup, which tends to <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>> as
  <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>>. Given its terminal value, the interacting field is
  still the Gaussian bridge, as the density of <math|\<bbb-P\>> with respect
  to <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>> depends on <math|X<rsub|T>> only:

  <\equation>
    \<Phi\><rsub|t>=J<rsub|t>\<Phi\><rsub|T>+\<zeta\><rsub|t>,<space|1em>\<zeta\><rsub|t>\<sim\>
    N<around|(|0,Q<rsub|t><around|(|1-J<rsub|t>|)>|)>,<space|1em><text|independent
    of >\<Phi\><rsub|T>.<label|eq:bridge>
  </equation>

  Read<nbsp><eqref|eq:bridge> as a statistical model. The <em|signal>
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> is hidden, with <em|prior> <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>,
  and the path <math|<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|s>|)><rsub|s\<leqslant\>t>> is an
  <em|observation> of it: a copy attenuated by <math|J<rsub|t>> and corrupted
  by a Gaussian noise independent of the signal. Infinitesimally, the bridge
  is the Doob transform of <math|X> by the Gaussian density of the remaining
  increment <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>-\<Phi\><rsub|t>\<sim\>\<mu\><rsup|S<rsub|t>>>,
  so that

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:observation>\<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>-\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>B<rsub|t>,
  </equation>

  with <math|B> a Brownian motion independent of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>>, in
  the filtration enlarged by <math|\<sigma\><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>>: at
  each resolution the field is pulled towards the hidden terminal field, and
  the pull is seen through noise. The <em|filtering problem> is to estimate
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> from <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>=\<sigma\><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|s>:s\<leqslant\>t|)>>,
  the filtration of the Polchinski process itself. Let
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>>,
  <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>=<math-up|Cov><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>\|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|)>>,
  and let

  <\equation*>
    \<cal-R\><rsub|t>\<assign\>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>-Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<cal-R\><rsub|0>=0,<space|2em><wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\>0,
  </equation*>

  be the <strong|regulator>.

  <\key-result>
    <\proposition>
      <dueto|Filtering, formal; a theorem at finite
      cutoff><label|prop:filtering>

      <\enumerate-alpha>
        <item>The conditional law of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> given
        <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>> is the Bayesian posterior

        <\equation>
          <label|eq:posterior>\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>><around|(|\<mathd\>y|)>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<Phi\><rsub|t>,y|\<rangle\>>-<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y,\<cal-R\><rsub|t>y|\<rangle\>>>\<nu\><rsub|T><around|(|\<mathd\>y|)>.
        </equation>

        It depends on the path only through <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>, a
        sufficient statistic, and <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>> is the precision
        gained on <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>>.

        <item>The Brownian motion <math|W> of the FBSDE<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde>
        is the <strong|innovation> of the filter,

        <\equation*>
          W<rsub|t>=B<rsub|t>+<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>S<rsub|s><rsup|-1><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>-\<psi\><rsub|s>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s,
        </equation*>

        and the force is the posterior mean of the pull
        in<nbsp><eqref|eq:observation>, per unit
        <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>:

        <\equation*>
          F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>-\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>|]>,<space|2em><text|i.e.><space|1em>\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>.
        </equation*>

        So<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> is the observation
        equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:observation> written in its own filtration.

        <item>Every observable <math|O<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>>
        solves the filter equation

        <\equation*>
          \<mathd\>O<rsub|t>=<around|\<langle\>|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t><around|(|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>,\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>,
        </equation*>

        in particular

        <\equation*>
          \<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|t>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>.
        </equation*>
      </enumerate-alpha>
    </proposition>
  </key-result>

  <\proof>
    (a) By Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marginals> the conditional law is
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>>, the tilt of
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|S<rsub|t>><around|(|\<cdummy\>-\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>> by
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>>>. The ratio of the Gaussian densities of
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|S<rsub|t>><around|(|\<cdummy\>-x|)>> and
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>>> at <math|y> is
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>x,y|\<rangle\>>-<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y,\<cal-R\><rsub|t>y|\<rangle\>>>>
    up to a function of <math|x>; equivalently, apply Bayes' formula to the
    likelihood of<nbsp><eqref|eq:bridge>. (b) Integrate<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde>
    from <math|t> to <math|T> and condition on <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>>:
    since <math|F> is a martingale, <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><big|int><rsub|t><rsup|T><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s>F<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    s=S<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>>, so <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>>,
    and the posterior mean of the drift of<nbsp><eqref|eq:observation> is
    <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>-\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>F<rsub|t>>.
    Comparing the two expressions<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde>
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:observation> of <math|\<mathd\>\<Phi\><rsub|t>> gives
    the formula for <math|W>. (c) <math|O<rsub|t>=G<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>
    with <math|G<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|x><around|(|G|)>>
    is a martingale, so the drift in Itô's formula vanishes, and
    differentiating<nbsp><eqref|eq:posterior> in <math|x> gives
    <math|\<mathD\>G<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><math-up|Cov><rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>,G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|)>>.
  </proof>

  So the Polchinski process is a filter. The interacting measure is the prior
  on a hidden field; at resolution <math|t> one has observed the coarse field
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>, and <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>> is
  what is known about the fine one. The forecast <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>> is
  the posterior mean of the hidden field, and
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>F<rsub|t>> is the posterior mean of its pull
  on the observed one. For the Gaussian prior <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>>>
  the increments are independent, so the posterior mean is
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>> itself and <math|F=0>: <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>> is the
  estimate that ignores the interaction and <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>> the one
  that includes it. The effective potential is a likelihood ratio: by the
  proof of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:fbsde>,
  <math|Z<rsub|T><rsup|-1>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>>
  is the likelihood ratio of the observation up to resolution <math|t> under
  the interacting and under the Gaussian prior, and the Polchinski equation
  is the equation of its logarithm.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|Backward in resolution><label|prop:reversal>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> Under <math|\<bbb-P\>> the reversed
    process <math|<wide|\<Phi\>|^><rsub|s>\<assign\>\<Phi\><rsub|T-s>>,
    <math|s\<in\><around|[|0,T|]>>, solves the linear equation

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:reversal>\<mathd\><wide|\<Phi\>|^><rsub|s>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|T-s>Q<rsub|T-s><rsup|-1><wide|\<Phi\>|^><rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>s+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|T-s><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\><wide|W|^><rsub|s>,<space|2em><wide|\<Phi\>|^><rsub|0>=\<Phi\><rsub|T>\<sim\>\<nu\><rsub|T>,
    </equation>

    with <math|<wide|W|^>> a Brownian motion independent of
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>>, and its solution is<nbsp><eqref|eq:bridge>.
    Backward in resolution, the Polchinski process is a linear Markov
    process, with Gaussian transition kernels which do not depend on the
    interaction, started from the interacting measure. It is not a Gaussian
    process: its marginals <math|\<nu\><rsub|t>> are not Gaussian.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    Under <math|\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>>, <math|X> is a centred Gaussian process
    with independent increments, so its reversal is a Gaussian Markov
    process: for <math|t\<less\>u>, the law of <math|X<rsub|t>> given
    <math|<around|(|X<rsub|r>|)><rsub|r\<geqslant\>u>> is
    <math|N<around|(|Q<rsub|t>Q<rsub|u><rsup|-1>X<rsub|u>,Q<rsub|t>-Q<rsub|t>Q<rsub|u><rsup|-1>Q<rsub|t>|)>>,
    and letting <math|u\<downarrow\>t> gives the drift and the diffusion
    of<nbsp><eqref|eq:reversal>. Since <math|\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\>/\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\><rsub|0>>
    is a function of <math|X<rsub|T>>, the initial value of the reversed
    process, these transition kernels are the same under <math|\<bbb-P\>>,
    and only the initial law changes, from <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>>> to
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>.
  </proof>

  <paragraph|Generation by denoising> So two dynamics have the same marginals
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|t>>. Backward in resolution, the interacting field is
  progressively blurred by Gaussian noise,
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>=J<rsub|t>\<Phi\><rsub|T>+\<zeta\><rsub|t>>, until
  nothing is left at <math|t=0>; this dynamics does not know the interaction.
  Forward, the Polchinski process undoes the blurring, and all the
  interaction sits in its drift. For <math|0\<less\>t\<leqslant\>T>, at
  finite cutoff, the time-reversal formula for diffusions says that the
  forward drift is minus the backward one plus
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>> times the score of the marginal: if
  <math|p<rsub|t>> is the density of <math|\<nu\><rsub|t>>,

  <\equation*>
    <wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>F<rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>Q<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<Phi\><rsub|t>+<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>\<mathD\>log
    p<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>,<space|2em><text|i.e.><space|1em>F<rsub|t>=\<mathD\>log
    p<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>+Q<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<Phi\><rsub|t>:
  </equation*>

  the force is the score of <math|\<nu\><rsub|t>> relative to that of the
  Gaussian reference <math|\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>>, as
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|t>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|t>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|t>>>
  requires. The forecast is the optimal denoiser, and Tweedie's formula

  <\equation*>
    \<psi\><rsub|t>=J<rsub|t><rsup|-1><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>+Q<rsub|t><around|(|1-J<rsub|t>|)>\<mathD\>log
    p<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>|)>
  </equation*>

  is again <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>>. This is
  the structure of score-based generative models (diffusion models), with
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> as the data distribution, the blurring as the noising
  process and the Polchinski process as the generating dynamics.

  <\remark>
    <dueto|Time reversal compensates the drift up to the
    score><label|rem:reversal-score> Write both dynamics in the direction of
    increasing resolution. Forward, <math|\<Phi\>> has drift
    <math|b<rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>>, which contains the
    whole interaction. Backward, by<nbsp><eqref|eq:reversal>, the reversed
    process has at <math|s=T-t> the drift
    <math|\<beta\><rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=-<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*Q<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x>,
    which does not see the interaction at all. The two drifts do not cancel,
    and the time-reversal formula<nbsp><cite|And82|HP86> says what is left:

    <\equation*>
      b<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>+\<beta\><rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\>log
      p<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,
    </equation*>

    <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>> times the score of the density
    <math|p<rsub|t>> of the field at that scale. Reversing the direction of
    resolution compensates the drift exactly up to the score. For the free
    field <math|b<rsub|t>=0> and <math|\<mathD\>log
    p<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=-Q<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x>, so the whole backward
    drift is the Gaussian score; with an interaction, the score carries
    everything that the backward dynamics ignores. In Nelson's stochastic
    mechanics<nbsp><cite|Nel67> the backward drift, seen as a velocity, is
    <math|b<rsub|\<ast\>>=-\<beta\>>, and the half difference
    <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|(|b-b<rsub|\<ast\>>|)>=<frac|1|2>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\>log
    p<rsub|t>> is the <em|osmotic velocity>. The same score is the noise of
    the field equation of Lecture<nbsp>5, the observable
    <math|\<eta\><rsub|t>=K*\<Phi\><rsub|t>-J<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>> of
    Section<nbsp><hlink|9.3|cqft_lecture_5.tm#sec:DS> there:
    by<nbsp><hlink|(9.14)|cqft_lecture_5.tm#eq:noise-score> of
    Lecture<nbsp>5,

    <\equation*>
      \<eta\><rsub|t>=-J<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>log
      p<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=-J<rsub|t>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|b<rsub|t>+\<beta\><rsub|t>|)><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>,
    </equation*>

    so the noise at resolution <math|t> is, up to the smoothing
    <math|J<rsub|t>>, the part of the drift that time reversal does not
    compensate.
  </remark>

  <\remark>
    <dueto|The measurement coordinate and the
    literature><label|rem:measurement> In the literature on stochastic
    localisation<nbsp><cite|Eld13|Eld23|BBD24> the observation is written in
    another coordinate. Let <math|j<rsub|t>\<assign\>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<Phi\><rsub|t>>.
    Since <math|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>J<rsub|t>=\<cal-R\><rsub|t>>,<nbsp><eqref|eq:bridge>
    becomes a linear measurement of the terminal field with signal-to-noise
    ratio <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>>:

    <\equation>
      j<rsub|t>=\<cal-R\><rsub|t>\<Phi\><rsub|T>+N<rsub|t>,<space|1em>
      N<rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<zeta\><rsub|t>,<space|1em>\<bbb-E\><around|[|N<rsub|s>\<otimes\>
      N<rsub|t>|]>=\<cal-R\><rsub|s\<wedge\> t>,<space|1em> N<text|
      independent of >\<Phi\><rsub|T>.<label|eq:measurement>
    </equation>

    In this coordinate<nbsp><eqref|eq:posterior> is an exponential family
    with natural parameter <math|j<rsub|t>> and log-partition function

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|t><around|(|j|)>=<math-up|log><big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|j,y|\<rangle\>>-<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y,\<cal-R\><rsub|t>y|\<rangle\>>>\<nu\><rsub|T><around|(|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
      y|)>,
    </equation*>

    and

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|t><around|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>x|)>=<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|x,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>x|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>
    </equation*>

    up to a function of <math|t>. Then <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<mathD\>\<cal-K\><rsub|t><around|(|j<rsub|t>|)>>,
    <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>=\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-K\><rsub|t><around|(|j<rsub|t>|)>>,
    <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> j<rsub|t>=<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>\<psi\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    t+<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    W<rsub|t>> with the same innovation <math|W>, and

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:K-HJ>\<partial\><rsub|t>\<cal-K\><rsub|t>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-K\><rsub|t>|)>+<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>\<cal-K\><rsub|t>,<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>\<mathD\>\<cal-K\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>=0,
    </equation>

    the Polchinski equation in the variable <math|j><nbsp><cite|BBD24>
    (<math|\<partial\><rsub|t>\<cal-K\>=-<frac|1|2>\<bbb-E\><around|\<langle\>|y,<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>>y|\<rangle\>>>
    and <math|<math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>>\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-K\>|)>=\<bbb-E\><around|\<langle\>|y,<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>>y|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    expectations under the posterior). The basic form of Eldan's scheme is
    the profile <math|S<rsub|t>=<around|(|Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>+t|)><rsup|-1>>,
    <math|t\<in\><around|[|0,\<infty\>|)>> (the terminal resolution is then
    <math|t=\<infty\>>, and <math|Q<rsub|T>> is the cutoff covariance), where
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>=t> and <math|j<rsub|t>=t\<Phi\><rsub|T>+N<rsub|t>>
    with <math|N> a Brownian motion; Eldan's original
    construction<nbsp><cite|Eld13> uses an adapted control matrix. This is
    the profile of Lectures<nbsp>6\U7.
  </remark>

  <paragraph|Literature: Polchinski, Eldan and diffusion models> The objects
  of this section have been studied in three communities. Stochastic
  localisation was introduced by Eldan<nbsp><cite|Eld13> for the KLS
  conjecture and is surveyed in<nbsp><cite|Eld23>; Chen and
  Eldan<nbsp><cite|CE22> made it a general framework, the localisation
  schemes, for mixing bounds of Markov chains. That Eldan's process and the
  Polchinski flow of the renormalisation group are the same object in
  different coordinates is explained in the survey of Bauerschmidt, Bodineau
  and Dagallier<nbsp><cite|BBD24>, who use the Polchinski flow to prove
  log-Sobolev inequalities. On the generative side, diffusion models were
  introduced in<nbsp><cite|SWMG15|HJA20> and given their SDE form, a noising
  diffusion reversed in time by a drift built from the score,
  in<nbsp><cite|SSK21>; the time-reversal formula goes back to
  Anderson<nbsp><cite|And82> and Haussmann and Pardoux<nbsp><cite|HP86>, and
  Tweedie's formula for the posterior mean is discussed in<nbsp><cite|Efr11>.
  The two sides were connected by El<nbsp>Alaoui, Montanari and
  Sellke<nbsp><cite|EMS22>, who run stochastic localisation as a sampling
  algorithm, by El<nbsp>Alaoui and Montanari<nbsp><cite|AM21>, who read it as
  the observation of a signal in Gaussian noise (the filtering point of view
  of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:filtering>), and by
  Montanari<nbsp><cite|Mon23>, who presents denoising diffusions as instances
  of stochastic localisation. On the physics side, Cotler and Rezchikov
  showed that Polchinski's exact renormalisation group is a gradient flow of
  the relative entropy in the sense of optimal transport<nbsp><cite|CR23>,
  and proposed to read diffusion models as inverse renormalisation group
  flows<nbsp><cite|CR23b>.

  <subsection|Where the three views meet><label|sec:meet>

  The three constructions give one process. Renormalisation produces it as
  the field at resolution <math|t> with its effective drift
  (Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:fbsde>), control as the optimal state
  (<reference|eq:EL>), and filtering as the observation written in its own
  filtration (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:filtering>). Each of them has
  a name for each object:

  <\padded-center>
    <with|font-size|0.84|<wide-tabular|<tformat|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-valign|c>|<cwith|1|-1|2|4|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-width|0.2par>|<cwith|1|-1|1|1|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|2|4|cell-width|0.26par>|<cwith|1|-1|2|4|cell-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-tborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|9|9|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<table|<row|<\cell>
      \;
    </cell>|<\cell>
      <strong|Renormalisation>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      <strong|Control>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      <strong|Filtering>
    </cell>>|<row|<\cell>
      <math|t>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      resolved covariance <math|Q<rsub|t>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      time of the problem
    </cell>|<\cell>
      information gained <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>>
    </cell>>|<row|<\cell>
      <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      field at resolution <math|t>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      state
    </cell>|<\cell>
      observation; posterior mean for the Gaussian prior
    </cell>>|<row|<\cell>
      <math|F<rsub|t>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      effective force
    </cell>|<\cell>
      costate
    </cell>|<\cell>
      posterior mean of the terminal force
    </cell>>|<row|<\cell>
      <math|W>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      fresh fluctuation of the shell
    </cell>|<\cell>
      noise of the controlled field
    </cell>|<\cell>
      innovation
    </cell>>|<row|<\cell>
      <math|S<rsub|t>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      unresolved covariance
    </cell>|<\cell>
      free motion from <math|t> to <math|T>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      posterior covariance for the Gaussian prior
    </cell>>|<row|<\cell>
      <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      mean of the conditional law <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      terminal state predicted by free motion
    </cell>|<\cell>
      posterior mean
    </cell>>|<row|<\cell>
      <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      covariance of <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      <math|S<rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t>S<rsub|t>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      posterior covariance
    </cell>>|<row|<\cell>
      <math|V<rsub|t>>
    </cell>|<\cell>
      effective potential
    </cell>|<\cell>
      value function
    </cell>|<\cell>
      minus the log-likelihood ratio of the observation, up to <math|log
      Z<rsub|T>>
    </cell>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  In the last column, <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>> is the estimate of the terminal
  field that ignores the interaction, and <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>> is the
  estimate that includes it. Their difference is <math|S<rsub|t>F<rsub|t>>:
  the force is what the interaction adds to the estimate, in units of the
  free propagator <math|S<rsub|t>>. The conditional covariance
  <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>> replaces <math|S<rsub|t>> as the propagator.
  Forces, propagators and covariances are the common language of the three
  views, and the next section writes the system in it. Only the last row of
  the table, and the formula for <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>> in the control
  column, involve a potential.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|polchinski_0d.pdf>|1par|||><label|fig:polchinski-0d>>|A
  zero-dimensional Polchinski process (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:zero-dim>):
  <math|Q<rsub|t>=1-\<mathe\><rsup|-t>>, <math|T=5>,
  <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2>*x<rsup|4>-<frac|3|2>*x<rsup|2>>.
  Left: the effective force <math|F<rsub|t>=-V<rsub|t><rprime|'>> is almost
  linear at low resolution and develops the double-well structure as
  <math|t\<to\>T>. Centre: paths of the FBSDE<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde>, which
  split between the two wells (stochastic localisation). Right: histogram of
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> (<math|2\<cdot\>10<rsup|4>> paths) against the
  density of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>.>

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Quadratic interactions><label|ex:quadratic> Let
    <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|\<kappa\>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0>. (a) Show that
    <math|V<rsub|t><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,\<kappa\>*<around|(|1+\<kappa\>*Q<rsub|T,t>|)><rsup|-1>*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>+<math-up|const>>
    solves<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski>. (b) Show that <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> is
    Gaussian with covariance <math|<around|(|Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>+\<kappa\>|)><rsup|-1>>:
    for <math|Q<rsub|T>\<to\><around|(|m<rsup|2>-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-1>> this
    is the GFF of mass <math|<around|(|m<rsup|2>+\<kappa\>|)><rsup|1/2>>. (c)
    Write<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> explicitly. It is a linear FBSDE. Solve it,
    and check that <math|F<rsub|t>> is a martingale. <em|Moral:> the mass can
    be put either into the covariance or into the force.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Zero dimensions><label|ex:zero-dim> Take <math|\<cal-M\>> a point,
    <math|Q<rsub|t>=q<around|(|t|)>> scalar, increasing,
    <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|x|)>=\<lambda\>*x<rsup|4>-a*x<rsup|2>> with
    <math|\<lambda\>\<gtr\>0>, <math|a\<geqslant\>0> (a double well if
    <math|a\<gtr\>0>; Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:polchinski-0d> has
    <math|\<lambda\>=<frac|1|2>>, <math|a=<frac|3|2>>). (a)
    Write<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski> and solve it numerically for
    <math|q<around|(|t|)>=1-\<mathe\><rsup|-t>>. (b)
    Simulate<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> by first computing
    <math|V<rsub|t><rprime|'>> on a grid, and compare the histogram of
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> with <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<lambda\>*x<rsup|4>+a*x<rsup|2>-x<rsup|2>/2*q<around|(|T|)>>>.
    (c) Compute <math|V<rsub|t>> to second order in <math|\<lambda\>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Stochastic maximum principle><label|ex:pontryagin> (a) For the
    control problem<nbsp><eqref|eq:BD>, perturb an optimal control
    <math|u\<to\>u+\<varepsilon\>*v> and show that
    <math|p<rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|[|-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|X<rsup|u><rsub|T>|)>|]>>
    satisfies <math|u<rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>*p<rsub|t>>.
    Identify <math|p> with the force <math|F> of<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde>. (b)
    Show that <math|\<Upsilon\><rsub|t>=\<mathD\>F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Optimal drift>(a) Derive<nbsp><eqref|eq:EL>
    from<nbsp><eqref|eq:BD>, and check that the optimal <math|u> is a
    martingale up to the factor <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>>. (b) For
    the quadratic interaction of Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:quadratic>,
    compute the optimal drift and the value<nbsp><eqref|eq:BD> explicitly.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Cost of the leading drift>For <math|V<rsub|T>=<frac|\<lambda\>|12>*<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>:<rsub|T>>
    on <math|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3><rsub|L>>, show that

    <\equation*>
      <frac|1|2>*\<bbb-E\>*<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>:X<rsub|s><rsup|3>:|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>\<mathd\>s=<frac|\<lambda\><rsup|2>|12>*L<rsup|3>*<big|int>Q<rsub|T><around|(|z|)><rsup|4><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>z,
    </equation*>

    which diverges linearly in <math|T>. Relate this to the <math|k=4> term
    of<nbsp><hlink|(A.6)|cqft_lecture_5.tm#eq:second-order> of
    Lecture<nbsp>5.
  </exercise>

  <section|Force coordinates and potentials><label|sec:wetterich>

  The three views agree on the forward\Ubackward system
  (<reference|eq:fbsde-system>), which from now on is the primary object,
  with a martingale force <math|F<rsub|t>=F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>>,
  <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> F<rsub|t>=\<Upsilon\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
  W<rsub|t>>. We look for the coordinates in which it is simplest. They are
  suggested by the table above: the posterior mean <math|\<psi\>>, which we
  call the <strong|forecast>, and the force. They remove the predictable part
  of the forward dynamics, and they integrate exactly the bilinear (tree)
  part of the force flow.

  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:burgers>\U<reference|sec:moments> tell this
  story in terms of forces, propagators and covariances. The potential enters
  only through the terminal force and, for a gradient force, through the
  identification of the response with the conditional covariance; the system
  in force coordinates makes sense for a force that is not a gradient.
  Sections<nbsp><reference|sec:generator>\U<reference|sec:tree> tell it again
  in terms of potentials: the value function generates the canonical
  transformation to force coordinates, the Legendre transform connects it to
  the filter, and the equation for the force is the Wetterich equation for
  the effective average action <cite|Wet93|BTW02>.

  <subsection|The force and a Burgers equation><label|sec:burgers>

  Differentiating<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski>, the effective force
  <math|F<rsub|t>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>>, now as a function of the field,
  solves

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:burgers>\<partial\><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>=-\<mathD\>F<rsub|t>*<around|[|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>|]>-<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>F<rsub|t>|)>,<space|2em>F<rsub|T>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|T>,
  </equation>

  an infinite-dimensional, backward, viscous <em|Burgers equation>. The
  Cole\UHopf transform <math|F=\<mathD\>log u> linearises it, which is the
  statement that <math|u=\<mathe\><rsup|-V>> solves a heat equation. Two
  remarks on<nbsp><eqref|eq:burgers>:

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
    \\documentclass[tikz]{standalone}

    \\usepackage[T1]{fontenc}

    \\usepackage[sc]{mathpazo}

    \\usepackage{amsmath,amssymb}

    \\usetikzlibrary{calc,decorations.markings}

    \\tikzset{

    \ blob/.style={circle,draw,thick,fill=#1,minimum size=7mm,inner sep=0pt},

    \ blob/.default={black!12},

    \ vtx/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.5pt},

    \ prop/.style={thick},

    \ full/.style={double,double distance=1.3pt,thick},

    \ ins/.style={circle,fill=black,inner sep=1.5pt},

    \ cross/.style={circle,draw,thick,fill=white,inner sep=0pt,minimum
    size=2.4mm,path picture={\\draw[thick] (path picture bounding box.north
    west)--(path picture bounding box.south east) (path picture bounding
    box.north east)--(path picture bounding box.south west);}},

    }

    % external legs: \\legs{node}{angles}

    \\newcommand\\legs[2]{\\foreach \\a in {#2}{\\draw[thick]
    (#1.\\a)--++(\\a:2.6mm);}}

    \\newcommand\\rootleg[2]{\\draw[line width=1.4pt,-latex]
    (#1.#2)--++(#2:3.6mm);}

    \\begin{document}

