    \;
  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>>>|<label|fig:polchinski-graphs>The
  Polchinski equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski> and the Burgers
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:burgers> as graphs. A blob with <math|n> legs is
  the <math|n>-th derivative of <math|V<rsub|t>>, or of the force
  <math|F<rsub|t>>, whose root is the arrow; a line with a dot is the kernel
  <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>. The first term joins two blobs and makes a
  tree; the second closes a loop on one blob. Integrating from
  <math|V<rsub|T>> and iterating generates the connected Feynman graphs, with
  lines <math|Q<rsub|T,t>>.>

  <\enumerate-roman>
    <item>it uses only the force, which is a local quantity (<em|intensive>),
    while <math|V<rsub|t>> is <em|extensive>, of order of the volume. This
    matters in infinite volume (Section<nbsp><hlink|9.5|cqft_lecture_5.tm#sec:infinite-volume>
    of Lecture<nbsp>5);

    <item>the Burgers flows for different covariances commute, and the
    curvature vanishes, when <math|\<mathD\>F<rsub|t>> is symmetric, i.e.
    when <math|F<rsub|t>> is a gradient<nbsp><cite|BCG26>. For a non-gradient
    force, as for the general Wilson\UItô diffusions of<nbsp><cite|BCG26>,
    the flows do not commute.
  </enumerate-roman>

  The function <math|F<rsub|t><around|(|\<cdummy\>|)>> with
  <math|F<rsub|t>=F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>> is the
  <em|decoupling field> of the FBSDE, and the Burgers
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:burgers> is its quasilinear PDE;
  <math|\<Upsilon\><rsub|t>=\<mathD\>F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>>
  is the martingale representation integrand of the costate. The two terms
  of<nbsp><eqref|eq:burgers> have distinct origins. The transport term
  <math|-\<mathD\>F*<around|[|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*F|]>> comes from the drift of
  the state, and generates the tree diagrams. The diffusion term
  <math|-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>F|)>>
  is the Itô correction, and generates the loops.

  <subsection|Removing the predictable drift>

  The drift <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>> of the state is
  predictable, and the costate is a martingale. Hence the drift that remains
  to be accumulated on <math|<around|[|t,T|]>> is predicted, at resolution
  <math|t>, by transporting the current state with the current costate along
  the free flow<nbsp><eqref|eq:shear>.

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The forecast><label|prop:psi> <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> The
    conditional mean of the terminal field is

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:psi>\<psi\><rsub|t>\<assign\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>,
    </equation>

    i.e. <math|<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>,F<rsub|t>|)>=\<Sigma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>,F<rsub|t>|)>>.
    It is a martingale, with

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:psi-noise>\<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>*\<Upsilon\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>=<around*|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>F<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>|)>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>.
    </equation>
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    The identity is in the proof of Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:filtering>(b),
    and uses no commutativity. For the differential, the drift
    <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>> of <math|\<Phi\>> cancels
    against <math|<wide|S|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>>, and
    <math|S<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>=S<rsub|t>*\<Upsilon\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>>.
  </proof>

  So <math|\<psi\>> is a closed observable, the <em|field observable>
  of<nbsp><cite|BCG26>. The field splits as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:lambda-split>\<Phi\><rsub|t>=\<psi\><rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>:
  </equation>

  a martingale, minus the drift still expected on the unresolved scales,
  which vanishes at <math|t=T>. The noise of <math|\<psi\>>
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:psi-noise> has two sources: the fresh fluctuation of the
  shell, <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>>, and the
  revision of the forecast of the remaining drift,
  <math|S<rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>F<rsub|t>>. The coupling between the
  forward and the backward equations, which was in the drift of
  <math|\<Phi\>>, now sits in the volatility of <math|\<psi\>>. The
  coordinate <math|\<psi\>> is the <em|interaction picture> with respect to
  the noiseless (classical) part of the dynamics.

  <subsection|The FBSDE in the forecast coordinate>

  Assume that the map

  <\equation*>
    x\<mapsto\>\<psi\>=x+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>
  </equation*>

  is invertible, and write its inverse as

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:coord>x=\<psi\>+S<rsub|t>*\<cal-F\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>,<space|2em>\<cal-F\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>\<assign\>-F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>,
  </equation>

  so that <math|F<rsub|t>=-\<cal-F\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>>:
  the costate is now a function of the forecast. Introduce the <em|response>,
  and recall the <em|regulator> of the filter:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:response>H<rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>\<assign\><around*|<left|(|2>|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\>\<cal-F\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>|<right|)|2>><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<cal-R\><rsub|t>\<assign\>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>-Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>,<space|2em><wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<geqslant\>0.
  </equation>

  Differentiating the two maps <math|x\<leftrightarrow\>\<psi\>> gives

  <\equation*>
    <with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=<around|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>\<cal-F\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>|)><rsup|-1>=H<rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>.
  </equation*>

  <\theorem>
    <dueto|FBSDE in force coordinates><label|thm:transformed>
    <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> In the coordinates
    <math|<around|(|\<psi\>,\<cal-F\>|)>>, the
    system<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde-system> becomes

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:transformed-fbsde>\<mathd\>\<psi\><rsub|t>=H<rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>*<space|0.17em>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t><rsup|1/2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|t>,<space|2em>\<cal-F\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|<left|[|1>|\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|T>|)>|<right|]|1>>,
    </equation>

    with <math|\<psi\><rsub|0>=S<rsub|0>*F<rsub|0>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>>
    and <math|\<psi\><rsub|T>=\<Phi\><rsub|T>>. The forward equation has no
    drift, and its covariance is

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-C\><rsub|t>\<assign\>H<rsub|t>*<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t><rsup|\<ast\>>.
    </equation*>

    The decoupling field <math|\<cal-F\>> solves the pure diffusion equation

    <\equation>
      <label|eq:transformed-flow>\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<cal-F\><rsub|t>+<tfrac|1|2><space|0.17em><math-up|Tr><around*|<left|[|3>|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|<right|]|3>>=0,<space|2em>\<cal-F\><rsub|T>=\<mathD\>V<rsub|T>.
    </equation>
  </theorem>

  <\proof>
    The forward equation is<nbsp><eqref|eq:psi-noise> with
    <math|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>F<rsub|t>=H<rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>>,
    and its covariance is

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*H<rsub|t><rsup|\<ast\>>=H<rsub|t>*<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t><rsup|\<ast\>>.
    </equation*>

    The backward equation is <math|F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|F<rsub|T>|]>>
    with <math|\<psi\><rsub|T>=\<Phi\><rsub|T>>. Since
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>> is a martingale and
    <math|\<psi\>> has no drift, the drift in Itô's formula for
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>> is the left-hand
    side of<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-flow>.
  </proof>

  Compare<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-flow> with the Burgers
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:burgers>: the transport term has been absorbed
  exactly by the change of coordinates, and only the Itô term is left. If it
  is dropped, <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>=\<mathD\>V<rsub|T>> does not move: all
  the tree diagrams are contained in the coordinate
  change<nbsp><eqref|eq:coord>. The loops are generated only by the quadratic
  variation <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>> of the forecast, i.e. by the randomness
  of the noise and of the force revisions, and the nonlinearity has moved
  into the field dependence of <math|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>>. None of this uses a
  gradient structure or a Gaussian convolution:
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:transformed> holds for any FBSDE of the
  form<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde-system> with a Markov martingale force, gradient
  or not, for which the coordinate change is invertible. For a general
  terminal force <math|F<rsub|T>=\<frak-f\><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>>,
  replace <math|\<mathD\>V<rsub|T>> by <math|-\<frak-f\>>
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-fbsde> and<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-flow>.

  <paragraph|A one-dimensional model: Burgers and Lax\UOleinik> The passage
  to force coordinates is the passage from Eulerian to Lagrangian coordinates
  for Burgers' equation. Consider first the inviscid Burgers equation on
  <math|\<bbb-R\>>,

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|s>u+u*\<partial\><rsub|x>u=0,<space|2em>u<around|(|0,x|)>=g<rprime|'><around|(|x|)>.
  </equation*>

  Along the characteristics <math|x=y+s*g<rprime|'><around|(|y|)>> the
  velocity is constant, <math|u<around|(|s,y+s*g<rprime|'><around|(|y|)>|)>=g<rprime|'><around|(|y|)>>:
  in the Lagrangian label <math|y> the transport term disappears, and nothing
  moves. As long as the characteristics do not cross, i.e.
  <math|1+s*g<rprime|''>\<gtr\>0>, the map <math|y\<mapsto\>x> is invertible,
  and <math|u=\<partial\><rsub|x>G> with the potential given by the
  <em|Lax\UOleinik formula> (or Hopf\ULax formula)

  <\equation*>
    G<around|(|s,x|)>=min<rsub|y><around*|{|g<around|(|y|)>+<frac|<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|2>|2*s>|}>,<space|2em>u<around|(|s,x|)>=<frac|x-y<around|(|s,x|)>|s>=g<rprime|'><around|(|y<around|(|s,x|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  whose minimiser <math|y<around|(|s,x|)>> is the Lagrangian label of the
  point <math|x>. After the characteristics cross, the formula still selects
  one label, the entropy solution, and <math|u> has shocks. The viscous
  equation <math|\<partial\><rsub|s>u+u*\<partial\><rsub|x>u=\<varepsilon\>*\<partial\><rsub|x><rsup|2>u>
  is linearised by the Cole\UHopf transform,

  <\equation*>
    G<rsup|\<varepsilon\>><around|(|s,x|)>=-2*\<varepsilon\>*log
    <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-<around|(|g<around|(|y|)>+<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|2>/2*s|)>/2*\<varepsilon\>>*\<mathd\>y,
  </equation*>

  and <math|G<rsup|\<varepsilon\>>\<to\>G> as <math|\<varepsilon\>\<to\>0> by
  Laplace's method.

  The dictionary with this section is: the unresolved covariance
  <math|S<rsub|t>> is the time <math|s>, the field <math|x> is the Eulerian
  point, the forecast <math|\<psi\>> is the Lagrangian label <math|y>,
  <math|-F<rsub|t>=\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>> is the velocity <math|u> (and
  <math|V<rsub|t>> is <math|G>), and <math|V<rsub|T>> is the datum <math|g>.
  The shear<nbsp><eqref|eq:shear>, i.e.<nbsp><eqref|eq:coord> at tree level,
  <math|x=\<psi\>+S<rsub|t>\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>, is the
  characteristic map; the noiseless Polchinski equation is the
  Hamilton\UJacobi equation <math|\<partial\><rsub|s>G+<frac|1|2><around|(|\<partial\><rsub|x>G|)><rsup|2>=0>,
  whose gradient solves the inviscid Burgers equation; the tree-level
  potential<nbsp><eqref|eq:hopf-lax> is the Lax\UOleinik formula; and the
  condition for the characteristics not to cross,
  <math|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T>\<gtr\>0>, is
  <math|1+s*g<rprime|''>\<gtr\>0>. The Polchinski equation is the viscous
  Hamilton\UJacobi equation, with <math|\<varepsilon\>=<frac|1|2>> in the
  metric <math|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:Vt> is the
  Cole\UHopf formula.

  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:transformed> is the Lagrangian description of
  the <em|viscous> problem. With noise the label is no longer a function of
  the Eulerian point: it is its posterior mean, the mean <math|\<psi\>> of
  <math|y> under the Cole\UHopf measure <math|\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-g<around|(|y|)>-<around|(|x-y|)><rsup|2>/2*s>>,
  which is <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|x>>. In this label the velocity is no
  longer constant but a martingale, and its
  evolution<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-flow> contains only the loops. The
  Lax\UOleinik form survives exactly: the Moreau
  formula<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau> of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:generator> is
  the Lax\UOleinik formula with the datum <math|V<rsub|T>> replaced by
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>, and at finite cutoff no shocks form: the noise
  keeps the label map invertible, locally by
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:H-cov> and globally by the convexity
  argument of Section<nbsp><reference|sec:generator>.
  Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:shear> shows the construction for a double well.

  <subsection|Conditional moments><label|sec:moments>

  For the Polchinski process the response has a direct meaning.

  <\proposition>
    <label|prop:H-cov><with|font-shape|right|(formal)> If
    <math|F<rsub|t>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>> then

    <\equation*>
      H<rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>=<math-up|Cov><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>=S<rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>*S<rsub|t>.
    </equation*>

    In particular <math|H<rsub|t>\<gtr\>0> is symmetric,
    <math|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>F<rsub|t>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>>
    is always invertible, and <math|H<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|t>> when
    <math|V<rsub|T>> is convex.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By<nbsp><eqref|eq:localisation>, the law of <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> given
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>> depends only on <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>, so it is
    also the law given <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>. The conditional law
    <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>>> is the tilt of
    <math|\<mu\><rsup|S<rsub|t>>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-\<phi\>|)>> by
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>>>; differentiating twice in
    <math|\<phi\>> gives <math|<math-up|Cov><around|(|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<phi\>>|)>=S<rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>*S<rsub|t>>.
    On the other hand <math|H<rsub|t>*S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>F<rsub|t>=<with|math-font|Bbb|1>-S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t>>.
    The last claim is the Brascamp\ULieb inequality.
  </proof>

  So the forecast and the response are the first two conditional moments of
  the stochastic localisation <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>> of
  Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:marginals>:
  <math|<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>,<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>|)>=<around|(|\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Phi\><rsub|t>|]>,<math-up|Cov><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<Phi\><rsub|t>|)>|)>>
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:localisation>). They are tied by Itô's formula:
  since <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>\<otimes\>\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>=\<psi\><rsub|t>\<otimes\>\<psi\><rsub|t>+<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>
  is a martingale,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:cov-dynamics>\<mathd\><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>><rsub|t>=\<cal-C\><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t><wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t><math-up|Cov><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t,<space|2em><math-up|dCov><rsub|t>=-<math-up|Cov><rsub|t><wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t><math-up|Cov><rsub|t><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>t+\<mathd\><around|(|<text|martingale>|)>.
  </equation>

  Conditional variance is resolved at the rate
  <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t><wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t><math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>,
  which for the Gaussian reference is <math|S<rsub|t>*<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>.

  <paragraph|Brascamp\ULieb and Cramér\URao bounds> The conditional law
  <math|\<mu\><rsup|\<phi\>><rsub|t>> has density proportional to
  <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-\<cal-U\>>>, with
  <math|\<cal-U\><around|(|y|)>=V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>+<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y-\<phi\>,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|y-\<phi\>|)>|\<rangle\>>>
  and <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-U\>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T>>.
  Two classical inequalities bound its covariance by the Hessian of
  <math|\<cal-U\>>. If <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-U\>\<gtr\>0>, the
  Brascamp\ULieb inequality<nbsp><cite|BL76>,
  <math|Var<rsub|\<mu\>><around|(|f|)>\<leqslant\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>f,<around|(|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-U\>|)><rsup|-1>*\<mathD\>f|\<rangle\>>>,
  applied to linear functions <math|f=<around|\<langle\>|h,\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>>>,
  and the Cramér\URao inequality, which needs no convexity, give respectively
  the upper and the lower bound in

  <\equation*>
    <around*|(|\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>|)><rsup|-1>\<leqslant\>Cov<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around*|[|<around*|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|)><rsup|-1>|]>.
  </equation*>

  The lower bound follows from <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|<around|(|y-m|)>\<otimes\>\<mathD\>\<cal-U\><around|(|y|)>|]>=1>
  (integration by parts) and the Cauchy\USchwarz inequality, with the Fisher
  information <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|\<mathD\>\<cal-U\>\<otimes\>\<mathD\>\<cal-U\>|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-U\>>
  (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:brascamp-lieb>). The two bounds coincide when
  <math|V<rsub|T>> is quadratic, and their gap is a Jensen gap for the
  inverse. Through Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:H-cov> they are two-sided
  bounds on the Hessian of the effective potential, i.e. on the response
  <math|H<rsub|t>>, which is the propagator of the Wetterich equation below.
  Some consequences:

  <\itemize>
    <item>If <math|V<rsub|T>> is convex, then
    <math|Cov<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|t>>: the interaction can only reduce
    the conditional variance. Equivalently
    <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t>\<geqslant\>0>: convexity is preserved
    along the Polchinski flow (as also follows from the Prékopa\ULeindler
    inequality).

    <item>If <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T>\<geqslant\>\<kappa\>> with
    <math|\<kappa\>\<gtr\>0>, then <math|Cov<rsub|t>\<leqslant\><around|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<kappa\>|)><rsup|-1>>
    uniformly in the conditioning: a uniform mass in the interaction is a
    uniform mass in every conditional law.

    <item>For <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> convexity is already lost
    through the Wick ordering: <math|H<rsub|4>*<around|(|\<varphi\>;c<rsub|T>|)>=\<varphi\><rsup|4>-6*c<rsub|T>*\<varphi\><rsup|2>+3*c<rsub|T><rsup|2>>,
    so<nbsp><eqref|eq:phi4-VT> has curvature
    <math|-\<lambda\>*c<rsub|T>+2*\<gamma\><rsub|T>> at <math|\<varphi\>=0>,
    which diverges as <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> (this happens already for
    <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|2>>). The upper bound is lost, and at low
    resolution the conditional law may spread over several wells
    (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:localisation>). This is the large-field
    problem. In Lecture<nbsp>6 it is handled by a clipped approximate force,
    whose global coercivity (Lemma<nbsp>12.1 there) is a bound of
    Brascamp\ULieb type on the response.
  </itemize>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|localisation.pdf>|1par|||><label|fig:localisation>>|Stochastic
  localisation for a two-component field:
  <math|Q<rsub|t>=<around|(|1-\<mathe\><rsup|-t>|)>*<with|math-font|Bbb|1>>,
  <math|T=4>, <math|V<rsub|T>> a tilted double well, and the contours show
  the density of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>. One path of<nbsp><eqref|eq:fbsde> up
  to resolution <math|t> (navy), with its terminal value
  <math|\<Phi\><rsub|T>> (open circle). The forecast
  <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<Phi\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t>> (gold) is the
  mean of the conditional law <math|\<mu\><rsub|t><rsup|\<Phi\><rsub|t>>>:
  teal samples, and the ellipse is two standard deviations of
  <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>. At low resolution the conditional law covers
  both wells; as <math|t> grows it localises on one of them.>

  <subsection|Potentials: the value function as a
  generator><label|sec:generator>

  We now use that the force is a gradient,
  <math|F<rsub|t>=-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>>, whose graph is a Lagrangian
  submanifold, and that <math|V<rsub|t>> is the value function of
  Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:BD>. In Hamilton\UJacobi theory a value
  function is more than a cost: it generates a canonical transformation. A
  complete family of solutions of the Hamilton\UJacobi equation generates the
  transformation that trivialises the dynamics. Force coordinates are this
  transformation.

  <paragraph|Phase space and Lagrangian graphs> Pairs <math|<around|(|x,p|)>>
  of a field and a value of the force form the phase space
  <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>\<times\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>, with the symplectic
  form <math|\<omega\>=<big|sum><rsub|i>\<mathd\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>\<mathd\>p<rsub|i>>.
  A submanifold <math|L> of dimension <math|n> is <em|Lagrangian> if
  <math|\<omega\>> vanishes on it. The graph
  <math|L=<around|{|<around|(|x,F<around|(|x|)>|)>|}>> of a force field is
  Lagrangian if and only if <math|<big|sum><rsub|i,j>\<partial\><rsub|j>*F<rsub|i>*\<mathd\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>\<mathd\>x<rsub|j>=0>,
  i.e. <math|\<mathD\>F> is symmetric, i.e. <math|F> is (locally) a gradient:
  the gradient sector is the sector of Lagrangian graphs. A map that
  preserves <math|\<omega\>> (a <em|symplectic> map) sends Lagrangian
  submanifolds to Lagrangian submanifolds. The shear<nbsp><eqref|eq:shear> is
  symplectic, since <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t><rsup|\<ast\>>*\<omega\>=\<omega\>+<big|sum><rsub|i,j><around|(|S<rsub|t>|)><rsub|i*j>*\<mathd\>p<rsub|j>\<wedge\>\<mathd\>p<rsub|i>=\<omega\>>
  for <math|S<rsub|t>> symmetric. So it maps the graph of a gradient to a
  Lagrangian submanifold; if the image is again a graph over the new base
  variable, it is the graph of a gradient.

  <paragraph|The free action generates the shear> The cheapest way to reach
  the field <math|\<psi\>> at resolution <math|T> from the field <math|x> at
  resolution <math|t>, without interaction, is to minimise
  <math|<frac|1|2><big|int><rsub|t><rsup|T><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
  s> under the constraint <math|x+<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|T><wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>u<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
  s=\<psi\>>. The minimiser is <math|u<rsub|s>=<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|s><rsup|1/2>p>,
  with the constant costate <math|p=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><around|(|\<psi\>-x|)>>,
  and the minimal cost is the <strong|free action>

  <\equation>
    \<cal-A\><rsub|t><around|(|x,\<psi\>|)>=<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>-x,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><around|(|\<psi\>-x|)>|\<rangle\>>.<label|eq:free-action>
  </equation>

  The relation <math|\<psi\>=x+S<rsub|t>p> is the shear
  (<reference|eq:shear>). Since <math|\<mathD\><rsub|x>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>=-p>
  and <math|\<mathD\><rsub|\<psi\>>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>=p>, at fixed <math|t>

  <\equation*>
    <space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>=<around|\<langle\>|p,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|p,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    x|\<rangle\>>,
  </equation*>

  so <math|\<cal-A\><rsub|t>> is the generating function of the canonical
  transformation <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>>. Moreover

  <\equation*>
    \<partial\><rsub|t>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>=<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\><rsub|x>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>\<mathD\><rsub|x>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>:
  </equation*>

  for every <math|\<psi\>> it solves the noiseless Polchinski equation, so it
  is a complete integral of that equation, with parameter <math|\<psi\>>.
  Jacobi's theorem then says that in the new coordinates the Hamiltonian
  <math|H<rsub|\<ast\>>> vanishes: <math|\<psi\>> and <math|p> are constants
  of motion.

  This is what force coordinates are:

  <\padded-center>
    <math|<around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>,F<rsub|t>|)>> are the Jacobi coordinates
    of the free Hamiltonian of the field.
  </padded-center>

  The noise does not destroy this: the constants of motion become
  martingales, <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>> by Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:psi>
  and <math|F<rsub|t>> by Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:fbsde>. The only
  dynamics left is their quadratic variation.

  <paragraph|The value function generates the graph> The graph of the force
  is Lagrangian, hence so is its image under the shear. The image is a graph
  over <math|\<psi\>>: the map <math|x\<mapsto\>x+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>>
  is <math|S<rsub|t>> times the gradient of
  <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|x,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>>,
  which is strictly convex since its Hessian is
  <math|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1><math-up|Cov><rsub|t>S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<gtr\>0>
  (Proposition<nbsp><reference|prop:H-cov>), so the map is injective. Hence
  the image is the graph of a gradient:

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:F-gradient>\<cal-F\><rsub|t>=\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>,<space|2em>H<rsub|t>=<around*|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\><rsub|T>=V<rsub|T>.
  </equation>

  These two generating functions are related by the Moreau (infimal
  convolution) transform

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:moreau>V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=inf<rsub|\<psi\>><around*|{|\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|x-\<psi\>,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|x-\<psi\>|)>|\<rangle\>>|}>,
  </equation>

  whose optimality conditions <math|x=\<psi\>+S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>,
  <math|\<mathD\>V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>
  are<nbsp><eqref|eq:coord>. It is well defined by the same convexity: as a
  function of <math|j=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x>, the function
  <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|x,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>>
  is the Legendre transform of <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>>>.
  The value function <math|V<rsub|t>> itself need not be convex. We
  use<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau> to fix the additive constant in
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>. Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:shear> shows the
  construction in zero dimensions.

  At the minimiser, <math|V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>+\<cal-A\><rsub|t><around|(|x,\<psi\>|)>>:
  the value function is the cost <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> attached to the
  forecast, plus the cost of reaching the forecast by free motion. This is
  the rule for transporting a generating function along a canonical
  transformation:

  <\equation*>
    <space|0.17em>\<mathd\> V<rsub|t>=<around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|p,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>-<around|\<langle\>|p,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    x|\<rangle\>>=-<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|t>,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    x|\<rangle\>>,
  </equation*>

  since <math|\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=-F<rsub|t>=-p>.

  <paragraph|No convexity is needed> The construction uses no convexity of
  <math|V<rsub|T>>, <math|V<rsub|t>> or <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>. The convex
  function is <math|g<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|x,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>>:
  with <math|j=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*x> it is, up to a constant, the log-Laplace
  transform of the finite measure <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>-<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*y|\<rangle\>>>*\<mathd\>y>,
  for every <math|V<rsub|T>> bounded below. Its gradient in <math|j> is the
  conditional mean <math|\<psi\>>, and it is strictly convex because
  <math|Cov<rsub|t>\<gtr\>0>. In finite dimensions, when <math|V<rsub|T>> is
  finite, this exponential family is regular with full support, so
  <math|j\<mapsto\>\<psi\>> is a bijection of <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>: the
  image of the shear is a graph over the whole <math|\<psi\>> space, also
  when <math|V<rsub|t>> is a double well.

  For <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> one only gets semiconvexity,
  <math|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>=Cov<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<gtr\>0>,
  i.e. <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>+<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<cdummy\>,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*\<cdummy\>|\<rangle\>>>
  is strictly convex, which is what<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau> requires. At
  <math|t=0>, where <math|S<rsub|0>=Q<rsub|T>>, this semiconvexity is the
  convexity of the effective average action of
  Section<nbsp><reference|sec:legendre>, <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>>.
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> itself is convex if and only if
  <math|Cov<rsub|t>\<leqslant\>S<rsub|t>>, which holds for convex
  <math|V<rsub|T>> but may fail for a double well at low resolution.
  Convexity matters only without noise: the tree-level
  potential<nbsp><eqref|eq:hopf-lax> is merely semiconcave, and unless
  <math|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T>\<gtr\>0> the
  characteristics cross, producing caustics and shocks of the force. The
  noise removes them.

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|shear.pdf>|1par|||><label|fig:shear>>|The
  zero-dimensional model of Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:polchinski-0d> at
  <math|t=1.2>. Left: the shear <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>> (teal arrows) maps
  the graph of the force <math|F<rsub|t>=-V<rsub|t><rprime|'>> (navy) to a
  graph over the forecast <math|\<psi\>> (gold), which is the graph of
  <math|-\<Gamma\><rsub|t><rprime|'>>. Right: the bare potential
  <math|V<rsub|T>>, the effective potential <math|V<rsub|t>> and
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>, each shifted to have minimum <math|0>.>

  <subsection|The Legendre transform and Wetterich's
  equation><label|sec:legendre>

  <\key-result>
    <\theorem>
      <dueto|Wetterich equation<nbsp><cite|Wet93>><label|thm:wetterich>
      <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> solves

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:wetterich-Gamma>\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|<left|[|3>|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around*|<left|(|2>|<around*|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>-S<rsub|t>|<right|)|2>>|<right|]|3>>,<space|2em>\<Gamma\><rsub|T>=V<rsub|T>.
      </equation>

      Equivalently, the <em|effective average action>
      <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>\<assign\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>
      solves, up to a field-independent term,

      <\equation>
        <label|eq:wetterich>\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>=<tfrac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around*|<left|[|4>|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around*|(|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>+\<cal-R\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>|<right|]|4>>.
      </equation>

      It interpolates between the bare action

      <\equation*>
        \<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|T>=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>+V<rsub|T><around|(|\<psi\>|)>
      </equation*>

      (<math|\<cal-R\><rsub|T>=\<infty\>>) and the 1PI effective action
      <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>> of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>
      (<math|\<cal-R\><rsub|0>=0>).
    </theorem>
  </key-result>

  <\proof>
    Integrate<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-flow> in field space. With
    <math|\<cal-F\>=\<mathD\>\<Gamma\>>, the derivative of
    <math|H=<around|(|S<rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\>|)><rsup|-1>> in a
    direction <math|v> is <math|-H*<space|0.17em>\<mathD\><rsup|3>*\<Gamma\><around|[|v|]>*<space|0.17em>H>,
    so by the total symmetry of <math|\<mathD\><rsup|3>*\<Gamma\>>,

    <\equation*>
      \<mathD\><rsub|v><math-up|Tr><around*|(|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t>|)>=-<math-up|Tr><around*|(|H<rsub|t>*<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>\<mathD\><rsup|3>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|[|v|]>|)>=-<math-up|Tr><around*|(|\<cal-C\><rsub|t>*<space|0.17em>\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-F\><rsub|t>|)><around|[|v|]>.
    </equation*>

    Hence<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-flow> reads
    <math|\<mathD\><around*|(|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*H<rsub|t>|)>|)>=0>,
    and<nbsp><eqref|eq:wetterich-Gamma> holds up to a function of <math|t>
    alone. That this function is <math|-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*S<rsub|t>|)>>
    with the normalisation<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau> follows from the Polchinski
    equation by the envelope theorem (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:envelope>).
    For<nbsp><eqref|eq:wetterich>, note that
    <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>+\<cal-R\><rsub|t>=\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>+S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>>.
    For the endpoints: <math|S<rsub|T>=0>; and at <math|t=0>,
    <math|S<rsub|0>=Q<rsub|T>>, so<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau> identifies
    <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|x,Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*x|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|0><around|(|x|)>>,
    up to a constant, with the cumulant generating function of
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> at <math|Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*x>, whose Legendre
    transform is <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>>
    (Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:1PI>).
  </proof>

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<\script-output|tikz|default>
