  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>||||>>>|<label|fig:wetterich-graphs>The
  Wetterich equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:wetterich> as graphs. The double line is
  the full response <math|<around|(|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>+\<cal-R\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>=<around|(|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>+\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>>
  and the crossed circle is one insertion of
  <math|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>. There is exactly one loop: the
  tree term of Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:polchinski-graphs> has been
  absorbed by the Legendre transform. Each field derivative brings down a
  higher vertex, so the hierarchy does not close at a finite order. The
  bottom row expands the response around <math|S<rsub|t>>.>

  Thus the Wetterich equation is the gradient-integrated form of the force
  equation<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-flow>, i.e. of the backward half of the
  FBSDE in forecast coordinates<nbsp><cite|Mor94|BCG26>. Every object in it
  is an observable of the Polchinski process:

  <\padded-center>
    <data-table|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Wetterich>|<cell|FBSDE>>|<row|<cell|field
    argument <math|\<psi\>>>|<cell|forecast
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t><around|[|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|]>>, the
    state transported by <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>>>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|t>|)>>>|<cell|minus
    the costate, <math|-F<rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*<around|[|\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|T>|)>|]>>>>|<row|<cell|propagator
    <math|<around|(|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t>+\<cal-R\><rsub|t>|)><rsup|-1>>>|<cell|conditional
    covariance <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>>>|<row|<cell|regulator
    <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>-Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>>>|<cell|precision
    of the unresolved shell, minus that of the
    prior>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>>|<cell|<math|<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|[|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*<around|(|<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>|)>|]>>>>>>>
  </padded-center>

  The flow of <math|\<Gamma\>> is driven by the difference between the
  interacting and the free conditional covariances, paired with the shell
  <math|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><rsub|t>>. It is <em|one-loop exact>, with
  the full propagator inside the trace. In the Gaussian case
  <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> is constant and only the constant
  term of <math|\<Gamma\>> flows. For the heat profile with
  <math|T=\<infty\>>, <math|S<rsub|t>=C*<around|(|1-\<mathe\><rsup|-A/t<rsup|2>>|)>>
  and

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:exp-regulator>\<cal-R\><rsub|t>=<frac|A|\<mathe\><rsup|A/t<rsup|2>>-1>,
  </equation>

  which behaves like a mass term <math|t<rsup|2>> on the modes
  <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<ll\>t> (for <math|m\<ll\>t>) and is exponentially
  small on the modes <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<gg\>t>: the exponential
  regulator of the functional renormalisation group
  (Figure<nbsp><reference|fig:regulator>).

  <\proposition>
    <dueto|The Legendre transform, formal; a theorem at finite
    cutoff><label|prop:legendre>With <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|t>> as in
    Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:measurement>, the Legendre transform of the
    log-partition function of the filter gives the effective action:

    <\equation*>
      \<cal-K\><rsub|t><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>+<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>+<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<cal-R\><rsub|t>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>
    </equation*>

    up to a function of <math|t>, and differentiating at fixed <math|\<psi\>>
    gives the Wetterich equation (<reference|eq:wetterich-Gamma>) again.
  </proposition>

  <\proof>
    By (<reference|eq:moreau>) and the formula for <math|\<cal-K\>>. At fixed
    <math|\<psi\>>, with <math|j=\<mathD\>\<cal-K\><rsup|\<ast\>><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>,
    the envelope theorem and<nbsp><eqref|eq:K-HJ> give

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|t>\<cal-K\><rsup|\<ast\>>=<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><math-up|Cov>|)>+<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>,
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<partial\><rsub|t><frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,S<rsup|-1>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>=<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>>\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>.
    Hence <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>=<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>><math-up|Cov>|)>>
    up to a function of <math|t>, which is
    <math|-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|\<cal-R\>|\<dot\>>S|)>> with
    the normalisation (<reference|eq:moreau>).
  </proof>

  So one Lagrangian graph has three generators: <math|V<rsub|t>> in the field
  <math|x>, <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|t>> in the coordinate
  <math|j=S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>x> of Remark<nbsp><reference|rem:measurement>,
  and <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> in the forecast <math|\<psi\>>. The Polchinski
  flow is the Hamilton\UJacobi equation of the log-partition function of the
  localisation; the Wetterich flow is its Legendre dual<nbsp><cite|Mor94>,
  whose argument is the posterior mean, and whose regulator is the
  information gained. At <math|t=0>, where <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|0>=0>,
  <math|\<cal-K\><rsub|0>> is the generating function <math|\<Lambda\>> of
  the cumulants of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> below.

  <paragraph|Probabilistic meaning of <math|\<Gamma\>>> At <math|t=0> the
  effective average action is the 1PI effective action of
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>,

  <\equation*>
    \<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>+sup<rsub|j><around*|<left|{|2>|<around|\<langle\>|j,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>-\<Lambda\><around|(|j|)>|<right|}|2>>,<space|2em>\<Lambda\><around|(|j|)>=log
    <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|j,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>*\<nu\><rsub|T><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>,
  </equation*>

  the generating functional of the vertex functions introduced by
  Jona-Lasinio<nbsp><cite|JL64>. It is a probabilistic object, as
  Jona-Lasinio emphasised in his probabilistic reading of the renormalisation
  group<nbsp><cite|JL75|JL01>; for the link with large deviations see
  Section<nbsp>VIII of<nbsp><cite|JL01>. By Cramér's theorem the empirical
  mean of <math|N> independent samples of <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>> takes the
  value <math|\<psi\>> with probability

  <\equation*>
    \<approx\>exp <around|(|-N*<around|(|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0><around|(|\<psi\>|)>-V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>|)>|)>,
  </equation*>

  so <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>-V<rsub|0><around|(|0|)>> is
  the rate function of the large deviations of averages of the field. In
  particular <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>> is convex even when
  <math|V<rsub|T>> is not, it is minimal at the mean of
  <math|\<nu\><rsub|T>>, and its Hessian there is the inverse covariance.
  Formally the same holds for the field averaged over a large volume, with
  the volume as the large parameter and the effective potential of quantum
  field theory (<math|\<Gamma\>> per unit volume at constant <math|\<psi\>>,
  not <math|V<rsub|t>>) as rate function; in that limit flat parts may appear
  (the Maxwell construction).

  <big-figure|<with|par-mode|center|<image|<tuple|<#>|exp_regulator.pdf>|0.55par|||><label|fig:regulator>>|The
  regulator <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>> of the heat profile (<math|T=\<infty\>>,
  <math|m=1>): close to the mass term <math|t<rsup|2>> (dotted) on the modes
  <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<ll\>t>, exponentially small on the modes
  <math|<around|\||k|\|>\<gg\>t>.>

  <subsection|Variational meaning and the tree level><label|sec:tree>

  <paragraph|Constrained free energies> <with|font-shape|right|(formal)> By
  the Gibbs variational principle applied to the conditional
  law<nbsp><eqref|eq:localisation>, and the identity

  <\equation*>
    <math-up|Ent><around|(|\<nu\>\|\<mu\><rsup|S>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-x|)>|)>=<math-up|Ent><around|(|\<nu\>\|\<mu\><rsup|S>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-\<psi\>|)>|)>+<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|x-\<psi\>,S<rsup|-1>*<around|(|x-\<psi\>|)>|\<rangle\>>
  </equation*>

  for <math|\<nu\>> of mean <math|\<psi\>>,

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:constrained>\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=inf
    <around*|{|<big|int>V<rsub|T><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<nu\>+<math-up|Ent><around*|<left|(|2>|\<nu\><space|0.17em>\|<space|0.17em>\<mu\><rsup|S<rsub|t>>*<around|(|\<cdummy\>-\<psi\>|)>|<right|)|2>>:<space|0.17em><with|math-display|false|<big|int>\<varphi\>*<space|0.17em>\<nu\><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>=\<psi\>>|}>.
  </equation>

  So <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>, the effective average action up to the
  quadratic term <math|<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,Q<rsub|T><rsup|-1>*\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>>,
  is the free energy of the unresolved shell constrained to the forecast
  <math|\<psi\>>, and<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau> is the minimisation over the
  forecast. In the language of Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:BD>:
  <math|V<rsub|t>> is the value function of the control problem, and
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> is its Moreau transform, i.e. the value function
  expressed in the forecast coordinate, the interaction picture of the
  classical part of the dynamics.

  <paragraph|The tree level> Dropping the Itô term
  in<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-flow> gives
  <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>=V<rsub|T>>, and then<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau> becomes
  the Hopf\ULax (Lax\UOleinik) formula

  <\equation>
    <label|eq:hopf-lax>V<rsup|<math-up|tree>><rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=inf<rsub|y><around*|{|V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>+<tfrac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|y-x,S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|y-x|)>|\<rangle\>>|}>,
  </equation>

  the solution of the noiseless Polchinski equation
  <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*V=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>V,<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*\<mathD\>V|\<rangle\>>>.
  It is the saddle point of the Gaussian convolution<nbsp><eqref|eq:Vt>. Its
  minimiser solves the classical field equation
  <math|S<rsub|t><rsup|-1>*<around|(|y-x|)>+\<mathD\>V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>=0>
  in the shell <math|<around|[|t,T|]>> with background <math|x>, and
  expanding it in <math|V<rsub|T>> produces exactly the tree diagrams.

  <subsection*|Exercises>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Characteristics><label|ex:characteristics> (a) Show that the
    transport equation <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*F+\<mathD\>F*<around|[|<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*F|]>=0>
    is solved by <math|F<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=F<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>>,
    <math|y=x+S<rsub|t>*F<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>>, and that its
    characteristics are the trajectories of<nbsp><eqref|eq:shear>. (b) Verify
    that<nbsp><eqref|eq:hopf-lax> solves <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*V=<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>V,<wide|Q|\<dot\>>*\<mathD\>V|\<rangle\>>>,
    and that it is the limit <math|\<hbar\>\<to\>0> of <math|-\<hbar\>*log
    <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T>*<around|(|x+\<zeta\>|)>/\<hbar\>>\<mu\><rsup|\<hbar\>*S<rsub|t>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<zeta\>|)>>.
    (c) For <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\>*y<rsup|4>> in zero
    dimensions, solve the equation for <math|y> as a power series in
    <math|\<lambda\>>, and identify the coefficients as tree graphs.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Conditional moments>(a) Prove <math|<math-up|Cov><around|(|\<mu\><rsup|\<phi\>><rsub|t>|)>=S<rsub|t>-S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t><around|(|\<phi\>|)>*S<rsub|t>>.
    (b) Prove<nbsp><eqref|eq:cov-dynamics>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|A symplectic shear>(a) Show that <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>>
    preserves <math|<big|sum><rsub|i>\<mathd\>x<rsub|i>\<wedge\>\<mathd\>p<rsub|i>>
    iff <math|S<rsub|t>> is symmetric. (b) Show that the image of the graph
    of <math|-\<mathD\>V<rsub|t>> under <math|\<Sigma\><rsub|t>> is the graph
    of <math|-\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>, with <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>>
    given by<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau>. (c) For a non-gradient force, show that
    Theorem<nbsp><reference|thm:transformed> still holds, but that
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>> need not be a gradient.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The constant><label|ex:envelope>
    Differentiate<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau> in <math|t> at fixed <math|x>
    (envelope theorem), and use<nbsp><eqref|eq:polchinski> to show that the
    quadratic terms cancel and <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>*\<Gamma\><rsub|t>=-<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|t>|)>>.
    Deduce<nbsp><eqref|eq:wetterich-Gamma> with the stated constant.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Gaussian check>For <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=<frac|\<kappa\>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>,
    show that <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>=<frac|\<kappa\>|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<psi\>,\<psi\>|\<rangle\>>+<math-up|const>>,
    and solve the FBSDE<nbsp><eqref|eq:transformed-fbsde> explicitly. Compare
    with Exercise<nbsp><reference|ex:quadratic>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|1PI effective action><label|ex:1PI> (a) Let
    <math|\<cal-K\><around|(|j|)>=log <big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|j,\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|T><around|(|\<varphi\>|)>>\<mu\><rsup|Q<rsub|T>><around|(|\<mathd\>\<varphi\>|)>>.
    Show that <math|\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0>=\<cal-K\><rsup|\<ast\>>>,
    and that <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsup|<math-up|EAA>><rsub|0><around|(|\<psi\>|)>>
    is the inverse covariance of the tilted measure with mean <math|\<psi\>>.
    (b) Integrate<nbsp><eqref|eq:wetterich-Gamma> with
    <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> replaced by
    <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T>>, and obtain the one-loop formula
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>\<approx\>V<rsub|T>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr>log
    <around|(|<with|math-font|Bbb|1>+S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>V<rsub|T>|)>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Trees versus loops>Using<nbsp><eqref|eq:moreau>, show that
    <math|V<rsub|t>=\<Gamma\><rsub|t>-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>,S<rsub|t>*\<mathD\>\<Gamma\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>+O<around|(|\<lambda\><rsup|3>|)>>
    for the <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4>> interaction<nbsp><eqref|eq:phi4-VT>.
    Deduce from Proposition<nbsp><hlink|A.2|cqft_lecture_5.tm#prop:second-order>
    of Lecture<nbsp>5 that, to order <math|\<lambda\><rsup|2>>,
    <math|\<Gamma\><rsub|t>> is obtained from <math|V<rsub|t>> by removing
    exactly the term <math|k=1>, the one-particle-reducible tree; the bubble
    (<math|k=2>), the sunset (<math|k=3>) and the vacuum term (<math|k=4>)
    are 1PI and remain. Where does the sunset come from
    in<nbsp><eqref|eq:wetterich-Gamma>?
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Regulators><label|ex:regulators>Derive<nbsp><eqref|eq:exp-regulator>.
    Compute <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>> for the sharp Fourier profile, and for
    <math|<wide|Q|^><rsub|t><around|(|k|)>=<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>-<around|(|<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>+m<rsup|2>+t<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>
    (whose kernels are not smooth, but the formulas make sense). In the last
    case show that <math|\<cal-R\><rsub|t>=t<rsup|2>> when
    <math|T=\<infty\>>: a mass regulator.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|Brascamp\ULieb and Cramér\URao><label|ex:brascamp-lieb>Let
    <math|\<mu\><around|(|\<mathd\>y|)>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|-\<cal-U\><around|(|y|)>>*\<mathd\>y>
    on <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|n>>, with mean <math|m>. (a) Show that
    <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|<around|(|y-m|)>\<otimes\>\<mathD\>\<cal-U\>|]>=1>
    and <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|\<mathD\>\<cal-U\>\<otimes\>\<mathD\>\<cal-U\>|]>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-U\>>,
    and deduce <math|Cov<rsub|\<mu\>>\<geqslant\><around|(|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-U\>|)><rsup|-1>>.
    (b) For <math|\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-U\>\<gtr\>0>, prove the
    Brascamp\ULieb inequality for linear <math|f> by Hörmander's
    <math|L<rsup|2>> method: solve <math|L*g=f-\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>>*f> with
    <math|L=-\<Delta\>+<around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>\<cal-U\>,D|\<rangle\>>>
    and use <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|[|<around|(|L*g|)><rsup|2>|]>\<geqslant\>\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<mu\>><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\>g,\<mathD\><rsup|2>\<cal-U\>*\<mathD\>g|\<rangle\>>>.
    (c) In zero dimensions, with <math|S<rsub|t>=S> and
    <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>=\<lambda\>*y<rsup|4>>, compute both bounds
    numerically and compare with the conditional variance; repeat for the
    double well <math|V<rsub|T><around|(|y|)>=<frac|1|2>*y<rsup|4>-<frac|3|2>*y<rsup|2>>.
  </exercise>

  <\exercise>
    <dueto|The free action><label|ex:free-action>(a) Prove
    (<reference|eq:free-action>), and that
    <math|\<partial\><rsub|t>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>=<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|\<mathD\><rsub|x>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>,<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsub|t>\<mathD\><rsub|x>\<cal-A\><rsub|t>|\<rangle\>>>.
    (b) From the Moreau formula (<reference|eq:moreau>), recover the
    relations (<reference|eq:coord>) by differentiating
    <math|V<rsub|t><around|(|x|)>=\<Gamma\><rsub|t><around|(|\<psi\>|)>+\<cal-A\><rsub|t><around|(|x,\<psi\>|)>>.
  </exercise>

